\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces All ten do the same thing; how hard they do it differs by a factor of about 1\,700. On the small models the replacement was the runner-up --- the kind of error that looks like a typo. On Qwen3-4B the edit reached down to the 43rd candidate, which is the kind that looks like a hallucination.}}{2}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Same edit, same questions, ten models. Two of the three habits are essentially one model's. The left panel also shows how much the answer depends on where you draw the line: count truncations (\emph  {4417-B} $\to $ \emph  {4417}) as near misses and Llama is off the scale; require the character shape to survive and it is ordinary. The two right-hand panels are deterministic rules, not a judge --- open circles are zero.}}{3}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
